System Overview
The Cosmos-Encrypted Universe Mapping Engine (CEUME) is a distributed, fully encrypted cosmology framework designed to process and simulate petabyte-scale multi-messenger astronomical datasets (such as DESI spectra, Gaia astrometry, and LOFAR radio surveys) without exposing raw coordinate vectors to unverified cloud infrastructure or third-party institutions.
CEUME bridges high-performance distributed computing with zero-knowledge cryptography by coupling four core subsystems:
 * HEALPix Spatial Decomposition: Equal-area sky tessellation ensuring locality of cross-match and physics operations across distributed memory nodes.
 * CKKS Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE): Dynamic noise-budget and multiplicative depth control for secure, in-place tensor operations.
 * MPI-Based N-Body & MHD Solvers: Scalable gravitational and magnetohydrodynamic plasma solvers running over distributed cluster nodes.
 * Telemetry-Instrumented Verification Matrices: Rigorous tracking of energy conservation, momentum residuals, and crypto saturation limits.
Core Scientific Contributions
 * Encrypted Cosmology Runtime Enables end-to-end ingestion and secure cross-matching of massive astronomical catalogs (Gaia DR4 \leftrightarrow LOFAR) while preserving complete institutional data sovereignty.
 * Homomorphic Physics Engine Executes N-body gravitational force calculations and magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) plasma equations under strict CKKS multiplicative depth ceilings, incorporating automated key refresh and bootstrapping triggers.
 * HEALPix-Aware Domain Decomposition Maps MPI ranks to recursive equal-area sky tiles (N_{\text{side}} = 32), minimizing boundary halo exchange latency across celestial coordinates.
 * Configuration Curve for FHE Profiles Formalizes operational security tiers (SECURE_4, BALANCED_8, LAB_12, DEEP_16) governed by explicit multiplicative depth budgets (L_{\max}), balancing yield against computational overhead.
